      "content": "1. INTRODUCTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Acids produce hydrogen ions (H+) in water, taste sour, and turn blue litmus paper red.",
        "Bases produce hydroxide ions (OH-) in water, taste bitter, feel soapy, and turn red litmus paper blue.",
        "Neutralization is the chemical reaction between an acid and a base resulting in the formation of salt and water.",
        "The pH scale measures the concentration of hydrogen ions in a solution, where values below 7 are acidic and above 7 are basic.",
        "Common natural indicators include litmus (extracted from lichens), turmeric, and red cabbage extracts."
      ],
      "id": "09h4no1"
